---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
import MedalBadge from '../../components/MedalBadge.astro';

const allSystems = await getCollection('systems');

// Define other unique architecture systems
const systemOtherSlugs = [
  'ti-994a', 'mattel-intellivision', 'fairchild-channel-f', 'gce-vectrex', 
  'bally-astrocade', 'magnavox-odyssey-2', 'coleco-colecovision',
  'bandai-wonderswan', 'nintendo-virtual-boy'
];

const systems = systemOtherSlugs
  .map(slug => allSystems.find(s => s.slug === slug))
  .filter(Boolean)
  .filter(s => s.data.status !== 'vault')
  .sort((a, b) => {
    const tierOrder = { platinum: 0, gold: 1, silver: 2, bronze: 3 };
    const tierA = tierOrder[a.data.medal_tier] ?? 4;
    const tierB = tierOrder[b.data.medal_tier] ?? 4;
    if (tierA !== tierB) return tierA - tierB;
    return (b.data.total_lessons || 0) - (a.data.total_lessons || 0);
  });

const totalLessons = systems.reduce((sum, s) => sum + (s.data.total_lessons || 0), 0);
const totalGames = systems.reduce((sum, s) => sum + (s.data.total_games || 0), 0);

// Group by medal tier
const platinum = systems.filter(s => s.data.medal_tier === 'platinum');
const gold = systems.filter(s => s.data.medal_tier === 'gold');
const silver = systems.filter(s => s.data.medal_tier === 'silver');
const bronze = systems.filter(s => s.data.medal_tier === 'bronze');
---

<Layout title="Other Architectures Track | Code Like It's 198x">
  <main class="min-h-screen bg-gradient-to-b from-gray-900 to-black text-green-400">
    <div class="container mx-auto px-4 py-12">
      <header class="mb-12">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-4 mb-4">
          <a href="/tracks/" class="text-gray-400 hover:text-green-400 transition">
            ← Back to Tracks
          </a>
        </div>
        <div class="flex items-center gap-6">
          <div class="text-6xl">🔮</div>
          <div>
            <h1 class="text-5xl font-bold mb-2 text-transparent bg-clip-text bg-gradient-to-r from-teal-400 to-cyan-400">
              Unique Architectures
            </h1>
            <p class="text-xl text-gray-300">
              Exotic processors and specialized computing systems
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </header>

      <div class="grid md:grid-cols-4 gap-6 mb-12">
        <div class="bg-gray-800 rounded-lg p-6 border-2 border-gray-700">
          <div class="text-3xl font-bold text-white">{systems.length}</div>
          <div class="text-gray-400">Systems</div>
        </div>
        <div class="bg-gray-800 rounded-lg p-6 border-2 border-gray-700">
          <div class="text-3xl font-bold text-white">{totalLessons.toLocaleString()}</div>
          <div class="text-gray-400">Total Lessons</div>
        </div>
        <div class="bg-gray-800 rounded-lg p-6 border-2 border-gray-700">
          <div class="text-3xl font-bold text-white">{totalGames.toLocaleString()}</div>
          <div class="text-gray-400">Games to Build</div>
        </div>
        <div class="bg-gray-800 rounded-lg p-6 border-2 border-gray-700">
          <div class="text-3xl font-bold text-white">1976-2001</div>
          <div class="text-gray-400">Era Covered</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="bg-gray-800 rounded-lg p-8 mb-12 border-2 border-gray-700">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-4 text-white">About Unique Architectures</h2>
        <div class="prose prose-invert max-w-none text-gray-300">
          <p class="mb-4">
            Beyond the mainstream 6502, Z80, 68000, and x86 processors, the computing world was filled 
            with exotic and specialized architectures. These systems pushed boundaries, experimented with 
            new ideas, and created unique programming challenges. Each represents a different approach to 
            solving computing problems, from specialized gaming processors to innovative user interfaces.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-2 text-green-400">Pioneering Gaming Processors</h3>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 mb-4">
            <li><strong>Texas Instruments TMS9900:</strong> First 16-bit home processor with workspace concept</li>
            <li><strong>General Instrument CP1610:</strong> 16-bit console processor with unique instruction set</li>
            <li><strong>Fairchild F8:</strong> Early 8-bit processor with innovative channel architecture</li>
            <li><strong>MOS 6507:</strong> Cost-reduced 6502 variant with limited address space</li>
            <li><strong>Intel 8048:</strong> Single-chip microcontroller for embedded applications</li>
          </ul>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-2 text-green-400">Specialized System Concepts</h3>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 mb-4">
            <li><strong>Vector graphics:</strong> Mathematical display systems using direct beam control</li>
            <li><strong>Speech synthesis:</strong> Hardware-based voice generation and recognition</li>
            <li><strong>Cartridge computing:</strong> Instant-load software distribution</li>
            <li><strong>Light pen interfaces:</strong> Direct screen interaction before mice</li>
            <li><strong>Handheld processors:</strong> Ultra-low power computing for portability</li>
          </ul>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-2 text-green-400">Learning Value</h3>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
            <li><strong>Alternative paradigms:</strong> Different approaches to solving computing problems</li>
            <li><strong>Constraint programming:</strong> Maximizing capabilities within severe limitations</li>
            <li><strong>Historical perspective:</strong> Understanding why certain architectures succeeded or failed</li>
            <li><strong>Innovation principles:</strong> How radical ideas influence mainstream development</li>
            <li><strong>Specialized optimization:</strong> Programming for unique hardware characteristics</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="mb-12">
        <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mb-6 text-white">Learning Path</h2>
        
        {gold.length > 0 && (
          <div class="mb-8">
            <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
              <span>🥇</span>
              <span class="text-gray-300">Gold Tier - Advanced Unique Systems (2,048 lessons each)</span>
            </h3>
            <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-4">
              {gold.map(system => (
                <a href={`/systems/${system.slug}/`} class="block bg-gray-900 rounded-lg p-6 border-2 border-gray-800 hover:border-green-400 transition-all">
                  <div class="flex items-start justify-between mb-3">
                    <h4 class="text-lg font-bold text-white">{system.data.name || system.data.title}</h4>
                    <MedalBadge tier={system.data.medal_tier} size="small" />
                  </div>
                  <p class="text-sm text-gray-400 mb-3">{system.data.year || system.data.release_year} • {system.data.manufacturer}</p>
                  <div class="flex gap-4 text-xs text-gray-500">
                    <span>{system.data.total_lessons} lessons</span>
                    <span>{system.data.total_games} games</span>
                    <span>{system.data.estimated_duration}</span>
                  </div>
                </a>
              ))}
            </div>
          </div>
        )}

        {silver.length > 0 && (
          <div class="mb-8">
            <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
              <span>🥈</span>
              <span class="text-gray-300">Silver Tier - Specialized Exploration (1,024 lessons each)</span>
            </h3>
            <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-4">
              {silver.map(system => (
                <a href={`/systems/${system.slug}/`} class="block bg-gray-900 rounded-lg p-6 border-2 border-gray-800 hover:border-green-400 transition-all">
                  <div class="flex items-start justify-between mb-3">
                    <h4 class="text-lg font-bold text-white">{system.data.name || system.data.title}</h4>
                    <MedalBadge tier={system.data.medal_tier} size="small" />
                  </div>
                  <p class="text-sm text-gray-400 mb-3">{system.data.year || system.data.release_year} • {system.data.manufacturer}</p>
                  <div class="flex gap-4 text-xs text-gray-500">
                    <span>{system.data.total_lessons} lessons</span>
                    <span>{system.data.total_games} games</span>
                  </div>
                </a>
              ))}
            </div>
          </div>
        )}

        {bronze.length > 0 && (
          <div class="mb-8">
            <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
              <span>🥉</span>
              <span class="text-gray-300">Bronze Tier - Quick Exploration (512 lessons each)</span>
            </h3>
            <div class="grid md:grid-cols-3 gap-4">
              {bronze.map(system => (
                <a href={`/systems/${system.slug}/`} class="block bg-gray-900 rounded-lg p-6 border-2 border-gray-800 hover:border-green-400 transition-all">
                  <div class="flex items-start justify-between mb-3">
                    <h4 class="text-lg font-bold text-white">{system.data.name || system.data.title}</h4>
                    <MedalBadge tier={system.data.medal_tier} size="small" />
                  </div>
                  <p class="text-sm text-gray-400 mb-3">{system.data.year || system.data.release_year} • {system.data.manufacturer}</p>
                  <div class="flex gap-4 text-xs text-gray-500">
                    <span>{system.data.total_lessons} lessons</span>
                    <span>{system.data.total_games} games</span>
                  </div>
                </a>
              ))}
            </div>
          </div>
        )}
      </div>

      <div class="bg-gradient-to-r from-teal-900 to-cyan-900 rounded-lg p-8 border-2 border-teal-700">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-4 text-white">Recommended Learning Path</h2>
        <div class="space-y-4 text-gray-300">
          <div class="flex gap-4">
            <div class="text-2xl">1️⃣</div>
            <div>
              <h3 class="font-semibold text-white">Start with TI-99/4A TMS9900</h3>
              <p class="text-sm">Experience the first 16-bit home processor with its unique memory-to-memory architecture and workspace concept. Learn how different processor design philosophies affect programming.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="flex gap-4">
            <div class="text-2xl">2️⃣</div>
            <div>
              <h3 class="font-semibold text-white">Explore Console Gaming with Intellivision</h3>
              <p class="text-sm">Master the CP1610 processor and sophisticated controller system. Understand how gaming-focused hardware enabled new types of interactive entertainment.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="flex gap-4">
            <div class="text-2xl">3️⃣</div>
            <div>
              <h3 class="font-semibold text-white">Discover Vector Graphics with Vectrex</h3>
              <p class="text-sm">Learn mathematical graphics programming with direct vector beam control. Experience gaming before raster displays became standard.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="flex gap-4">
            <div class="text-2xl">4️⃣</div>
            <div>
              <h3 class="font-semibold text-white">Master Handheld Innovation</h3>
              <p class="text-sm">Explore ultra-low power computing with WonderSwan and Virtual Boy. Understand how portable computing drives different architectural choices.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="bg-gray-800 rounded-lg p-8 mt-8 border-2 border-gray-700">
        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold mb-4 text-white">Why Study Unique Architectures?</h3>
        <div class="text-gray-300 space-y-3">
          <p>
            Unique architectures teach fundamental lessons about computing that mainstream processors 
            often obscure. By working with constraints and unusual features, you develop problem-solving 
            skills that apply far beyond the specific systems. These platforms force creative thinking 
            and efficient programming practices.
          </p>
          <p>
            Each exotic architecture represents experiments in computing that influenced later designs. 
            The TMS9900's workspace concept influenced modern virtual machines. The Intellivision's 
            sophisticated controller design pioneered console gaming interfaces. Vector graphics systems 
            demonstrated alternatives to raster displays that remain relevant in modern graphics programming.
          </p>
          <p>
            Programming these systems provides historical perspective on why certain technologies 
            succeeded while others failed. Understanding these alternative approaches helps predict 
            future computing trends and makes you a more versatile programmer who can adapt to 
            any architecture.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="bg-gray-800 rounded-lg p-8 mt-8 border-2 border-gray-700">
        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold mb-4 text-white">Specialized Programming Concepts</h3>
        <div class="text-gray-300 space-y-3">
          <p>
            <strong>Constraint Programming:</strong> Many unique systems had severe limitations that forced 
            innovative programming techniques. The Atari 2600's 128 bytes of RAM taught generations of 
            programmers to maximize every bit. These skills translate directly to modern embedded systems 
            and mobile development where resources remain constrained.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Alternative Paradigms:</strong> Systems like the TMS9900's memory-to-memory operations 
            or the F8's channel architecture demonstrate radically different approaches to computation. 
            Understanding these alternatives expands your mental model of what computing can be and 
            prepares you for future architectural innovations.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Hardware-Software Integration:</strong> Unique architectures often included specialized 
            hardware that required intimate cooperation between software and hardware design. Learning 
            to program these systems teaches low-level optimization and hardware understanding that 
            remains valuable in performance-critical applications.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="bg-gray-800 rounded-lg p-8 mt-8 border-2 border-gray-700">
        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold mb-4 text-white">Innovation Lessons</h3>
        <div class="text-gray-300 space-y-3">
          <p>
            <strong>Risk and Reward:</strong> Many unique architectures represented bold bets on alternative 
            approaches to computing. Some, like ARM, became foundational to modern computing. Others, 
            like vector graphics, remained niche but influenced later developments. Understanding both 
            successes and failures teaches valuable lessons about technology adoption.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Market Dynamics:</strong> Technical superiority doesn't guarantee market success. 
            The TI-99/4A was technically advanced but failed commercially due to marketing decisions. 
            The Intellivision offered superior graphics but couldn't overcome the Atari 2600's 
            market dominance. These lessons remain relevant in today's technology landscape.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </main>
</Layout>